# The framework's own CI, its maintainer tools, its one-line installer and its
# security policy. A project made from this skeleton lives on a host we do not
# know (decision dec-projects-are-host-neutral, 2026-11-16) and has its own
# policy; carried along, these were dead files on GitLab, OUR release gates in a
# GitHub project's CI, and a security policy sending a product's reports to us.
# The repository keeps them: a workflow_call reads them from here, not from an
# archive.
/.github export-ignore
/tools export-ignore
/install.sh export-ignore
/SECURITY.md export-ignore
# The skeleton's own tests check files bin/semitexa update rewrites (the
# Dockerfile's Swoole pin, the compose files, bin/semitexa): shipped, each went
# red in a project the first time an update changed what it asserts (a
# consumer's SwoolePinnedTest after 2026.11.16.2053). They test the skeleton,
# not the product; the product keeps tests/Unit/Hello and tests/Integration.
/tests/Unit/Scaffold export-ignore
# This package's capability declaration: the workspace's capability catalog reads
# it from this repository (packages/semitexa-*/src/Capabilities.php). A project's
# src/ is its own: shipped, the skeleton's class sat in every project's src/
# (tk-init-command-lands-in-every-projects-src).
/src/Capabilities.php export-ignore
# The tests of the framework's CI and soak tooling read .github/ and tools/,
# which a new project does not get: shipped, its first test run would be red.
/tests/Unit/Ci export-ignore
/tests/Unit/Soak export-ignore
